{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Phase 1 RCA — confirmed root cause\n\nThe nested pytest invocation in tests/_store_isolation.py:35-42 omits --basetemp even though the outer helper owns a tmp_path. The child therefore joins pytest's user-global numbered temp root. At pytest session finish, TempPathFactory closes its cleanup stack (_pytest/tmpdir.py:213-225, 315-340), which synchronously scans and recursively deletes stale numbered trees (_pytest/pathlib.py:279-290, 335-369). That unrelated global cleanup can outlive this helper's fixed 90-second communicate timeout after the selected test has already reported [100%]. A killed cleanup can leave garbage-* for the next run to resume, explaining the full-suite timeout, the immediately repeated focused timeout, and the later unchanged 1.49-second pass. The changeable defect is the missing private --basetemp argument at tests/_store_isolation.py:35-42.\n\nDiscriminating proof:\n- Controlled real-filesystem A/B with 400,000 stale entries: shared-root child 10.800s; private --basetemp child 0.882s, with the poisoned shared root untouched. A 20,000-entry run showed the same direction (1.030s vs 0.551s), establishing cleanup cost scales with retained entries.\n- SIGUSR1 at the exact post-[100%] boundary captured the main thread in shutil._rmtree_safe_fd -> _pytest.pathlib.rm_rf:167 -> maybe_delete_a_numbered_dir:290 -> cleanup_numbered_dir:367 -> _pytest.tmpdir.pytest_sessionfinish:340; the process then exited normally after 13.107s. This observes the predicted mechanism executing.\n- Exact ambient-store semantics stressed 25 times with a unique child basetemp while a 200,000-entry shared root remained poisoned: 25/25 passed, min 0.579s, median 0.607s, max 0.972s, poison_unchanged=True.\n- Rival hypothesis: an unbounded repo-isolation git status can be fault-injected to reproduce the same output boundary, but 100 normal measurements were median 0.0143s, p95 0.0164s, max 0.0610s and no natural stall stack identified Git. The observed cleanup stack plus private-basetemp A/B rules it out as this incident's cause.\n\nDefect legitimacy: defect. The existing contract at tests/unit/test_test_suite_store_isolation.py and tests/_store_isolation.py:32-43 is that the selected node completes within the bounded child run while ambient tracker HEAD/directories remain unchanged. Letting unrelated user-global pytest retention work consume that budget violates the helper's isolation invariant; no intentional dependency on shared temp cleanup exists."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786891102587139001,"uuid":"0753b521-8cdf-47fe-ad5c-45ef8832acef"}